High-voltage direct-current transmission is an option for transmitting electrical energy over long distances, but it is not superior to AC transmission under every set of conditions.
In a simplified comparison, if the permitted DC operating voltage can equal the peak AC voltage and the current limits and other constraints are comparable, a DC line may carry more power;

using the same conductors alone does not guarantee this result.
Steady DC transmission avoids the inductive-reactance voltage drop and continuing capacitive charging current associated with AC lines, which matters for long-distance lines.

Inductance and capacitance still exist and affect changes in current or voltage;
DC transmission also retains conductor, corona, and converter losses.
